  "keyPoints": {
    "en": [
      "OS Chips must never be removed—removing your own OS Chip triggers Ending E immediately, losing all current progress to the last save point. Only remove OS Chip intentionally when targeting specific endings.",
      "The Chip system's weight limit constrains loadout options—prioritise Attack Up, ATB Acceleration, and Deadly Heal chips over defensive chips. Deadly Heal (restores HP on enemy kill) combined with Attack Up enables aggressive play without item management.",
      "Route B replays Route A from 9S's perspective with different gameplay sections—do not skip B thinking it is identical; approximately 40% of the content is unique to B including hidden cut-scenes and the true state of key characters.",
      "Emil's rotating shop (accessible by driving past his spawn location on the world map repeatedly) sells unique upgrade materials only available from him—visit Emil at least once per area transition to check stock.",
      "The Amusement Park machine enemies (Pascal's Village allies) reward higher-grade materials if you kill them without triggering alarm mode—approach from behind and initiate stealth kills for guaranteed rare drops."
    ],
    "ja": [
      "OSチップは絶対に外してはならない。自分のOSチップを取り外すとすぐにエンディングEが発動し、最後のセーブポイントへの全進行を失う。特定のエンディングを狙う時のみ意図的に外す。",
      "チップシステムの重量制限がロードアウトを制限する。防御チップよりも攻撃アップ、ATB加速、致命的な癒しを優先する。攻撃アップと組み合わせた致命的な癒し（敵撃破時にHPを回復）でアイテム管理なしの積極的なプレイが可能になる。",
      "ルートBはルートAを9Sの視点から再プレイするが異なるゲームプレイセクションがある。同一だと思ってBをスキップしないこと。コンテンツの約40%がBに固有で、主要キャラクターの真の状態についての隠しカットシーンを含む。",
      "エミールのローテーションショップ（ワールドマップ上の出現場所を繰り返し通過してアクセス）は彼からのみ入手可能なユニークなアップグレード素材を販売する。エリア移行ごとに少なくとも1回エミールを訪問してストックを確認する。",
      "遊園地の機械生命体の敵（パスカルの村の仲間）は警戒モードを発動させずに倒すと上級素材を報酬として与える。背後から近づいてステルスキルを開始し確実にレアドロップを得る。"
    ]
  },
  "opening": {
    "en": [
      {
        "title": "Combat System Fundamentals",
        "body": "2B and 9S have different combat toolsets: 2B has access to all weapon types and pod programs, while 9S can hack enemies for large damage bursts. In Route A (2B), build your chip set around raw attack speed and Deadly Heal—this eliminates the need to use healing items in most encounters. Learn Pod Program timing: the laser sweep (horizontal fire) covers 180° and is the most versatile AoE option; use it during enemy spawn animations when they cannot dodge."
      },
      {
        "title": "Hacking as 9S in Route B",
        "body": "Hacking enters a bullet-hell mini-game where you must destroy a core element to disable the hacked unit. Small enemies are destroyed in 3–5 hits of the hacking cursor; large machine enemies and bosses require multiple hacking phases. The critical advantage of hacking in bossfights: a successful hack on a boss deals approximately 30% of their maximum HP as flat damage independent of attack stats. Hack bosses whenever the hacking cursor turns gold (indicating full vulnerability)."
      }
    ]
  },
  "midGame": {
    "en": [
      {
        "title": "Chip Loadout Optimisation",
        "body": "The optimal Route A/B general chip loadout: Max Attack Speed (weight 4) + Deadly Heal ×2 (weight 3+3) + Attack Up ×2 (weight 3+3) + Counter ×1 (weight 2). This fits within the standard weight budget of 22 and provides the highest sustained DPS with self-healing. Upgrade chips using surplus chips of the same type: two Tier-1 chips fuse into one Tier-2 chip at the upgrade station, and Tier-2 fuses into Tier-3. Never discard duplicate lower-tier chips—always fuse them for upgrade progress."
      },
      {
        "title": "Side Quest Priority",
        "body": "Side quests in NieR:Automata are time-sensitive to Route progression—many quests expire after completing specific main story chapters. The Resistance Camp quests (Amnesia, A Lingering Resonance) and Pascal Village quests must be completed in Route A or they are replaced with different (lower-reward) versions in Route B. Priority quests: Amnesia (unlocks Pod Colour customisation), Speed Star (unlocks the fastest foot movement mode), and Find a Present (provides unique machine part upgrade for Emil shop discount)."
      }
    ]
  },
  "endGame": {
    "en": [
      {
        "title": "Route C, D, E and True Endings",
        "body": "Completing Route A and B unlocks Route C (A2's perspective plus final story). Route C has two ending choices at the final boss: Ending C (A2's path) and Ending D (9S's path). Both are required to unlock Ending E—the true conclusion. Ending E presents a bullet-hell challenge against developer credits; losing sends you back to the last checkpoint in Route C. The most important preparation: before entering Route C, max upgrade the Beastlord weapon set and carry 20+ High Speed Circuits for the crafting section."
      }
    ]
  },
  "mistakes": {
    "en": [
      "Accidentally removing OS Chip while reorganising loadout—keep OS Chip in a permanent slot and lock it to prevent accidental removal during chip management.",
      "Playing Route B as a content skip—Route B changes 2B's story to 9S's perspective and features unique hacking sections, a different ending to the Amusement Park sequence, and additional cut-scenes explaining key plot points.",
      "Ignoring the Fishing mini-game—fishing is not required but provides unique chip upgrade materials that are the most efficient source of high-tier raw chips before the endgame.",
      "Selling all looted items without checking the Emil shop requirements—Emil sells equipment that requires specific machine parts to unlock; keep at least 5 of each machine part until Emil's shop is fully unlocked.",
      "Entering the final chapter without maxing at least one weapon set—fully upgraded weapon sets unlock EX versions with bonus abilities; the EX weapon bonus is visible in the weapon description and is worth the upgrade investment before the final area."
    ],
